# mmio.orum — Orum v0.004 example
# Write a pattern through a `raw *u32` to a scratch address in a loop.
#
# COMPILE-ONLY on Linux: the scratch address below is not valid user
# memory, so DO NOT RUN this binary. Real MMIO addresses arrive with
# freestanding mode (a later version), where there is no OS to fault.
#
# What this proves: `rawptr` materializes a raw pointer from an integer,
# `p[i] = v` stores through it, `*p` dereferences it — and every access
# is volatile (the compiler emits one load/store per source access,
# never elided, merged, or reordered).

p: raw *u32 = rawptr(0xF00000)

# paint 16 registers with a walking pattern
mut i = 0
while i < 16
p[i] = i * 0x11111111
i = i + 1
end

# single-register write via dereference
*p = 0xDEADBEEF

# readback through the pointer
mut acc = 0
mut j = 0
while j < 16
acc = acc ^ p[j]
j = j + 1
end
